WEBVTT

00:00.670 --> 00:03.010
So we've got the
equation x squared

00:03.010 --> 00:04.975
plus y squared is equal to 1.

00:04.975 --> 00:07.120
I guess we could call
it a relationship.

00:07.120 --> 00:10.200
And if we were to graph
all of the points x and y

00:10.200 --> 00:12.220
that satisfied
this relationship,

00:12.220 --> 00:15.884
we get a unit circle like this.

00:15.884 --> 00:17.550
And what I'm curious
about in this video

00:17.550 --> 00:20.170
is how we can figure out the
slope of the tangent line

00:20.170 --> 00:23.260
at any point of
this unit circle.

00:23.260 --> 00:26.350
And what immediately might be
jumping out in your brain is,

00:26.350 --> 00:29.710
well a circle defined this
way, this isn't a function.

00:29.710 --> 00:32.890
It's not y explicitly
defined as a function of x.

00:32.890 --> 00:37.140
For any x value we actually
have two possible y's

00:37.140 --> 00:40.800
that satisfy this
relationship right over here.

00:40.800 --> 00:43.480
So you might be tempted
to maybe split this up

00:43.480 --> 00:46.450
into two separate
functions of x.

00:46.450 --> 00:51.530
You could say y is equal to
the positive square root of 1

00:51.530 --> 00:52.970
minus x squared.

00:52.970 --> 00:57.550
And you could say y is equal to
the negative square root of 1

00:57.550 --> 00:59.020
minus x squared.

00:59.020 --> 01:01.530
Take the derivatives of
each of these separately.

01:01.530 --> 01:05.317
And you would be able to find
the derivative for any x,

01:05.317 --> 01:07.400
or the derivative of the
slope of the tangent line

01:07.400 --> 01:08.622
at any point.

01:08.622 --> 01:10.080
But what I want to
do in this video

01:10.080 --> 01:12.970
is literally leverage
the chain rule

01:12.970 --> 01:15.060
to take the
derivative implicitly.

01:15.060 --> 01:17.850
So that I don't have
to explicitly define y

01:17.850 --> 01:20.434
is a function of x either way.

01:20.434 --> 01:22.100
And the way we do
that is literally just

01:22.100 --> 01:26.360
apply the derivative operator
to both sides of this equation.

01:26.360 --> 01:30.100
And then apply what we
know about the chain rule.

01:30.100 --> 01:32.970
Because we are not
explicitly defining y

01:32.970 --> 01:35.790
as a function of x, and
explicitly getting y

01:35.790 --> 01:38.130
is equal to f prime
of x, they call

01:38.130 --> 01:41.450
this-- which is really just an
application of the chain rule--

01:41.450 --> 01:43.185
we call it implicit
differentiation.

01:47.610 --> 01:50.440
And what I want you to keep
in the back of your mind

01:50.440 --> 01:52.730
the entire time
is that it's just

01:52.730 --> 01:54.440
an application of
the chain rule.

01:54.440 --> 01:57.710
So let's apply the derivative
operator to both sides of this.

01:57.710 --> 01:59.210
So it's the derivative
with respect

01:59.210 --> 02:08.220
to x of x squared plus y
squared, on the left hand

02:08.220 --> 02:10.270
side of our equation.

02:10.270 --> 02:13.020
And then that's going to
be equal to the derivative

02:13.020 --> 02:15.790
with respect to x on
the right hand side.

02:15.790 --> 02:17.810
I'm just doing the
same exact thing

02:17.810 --> 02:22.190
to both sides of this equation.

02:22.190 --> 02:25.660
Now if I take the derivative
of the sum of two terms,

02:25.660 --> 02:28.090
that's the same thing as taking
the sum of the derivative.

02:28.090 --> 02:29.631
So this is going to
be the same thing

02:29.631 --> 02:33.860
as the derivative with
respect to x of x squared,

02:33.860 --> 02:38.180
plus the derivative with
respect to x of y squared.

02:38.180 --> 02:40.329
I'm writing all my
orange stuff first.

02:40.329 --> 02:40.870
So let's see.

02:40.870 --> 02:43.914
This is going to be x squared,
this is going to be y squared.

02:43.914 --> 02:46.080
And then this is going to
be equal to the derivative

02:46.080 --> 02:47.660
with respect to x of a constant.

02:47.660 --> 02:49.650
This isn't changing
with respect to x.

02:49.650 --> 02:52.530
So we just get 0.

02:52.530 --> 02:54.260
Now this first term
right over here,

02:54.260 --> 02:56.720
we have done many, many,
many, many, many, many times.

02:56.720 --> 02:58.674
The derivative with
respect to x of x squared

02:58.674 --> 02:59.840
is just the power rule here.

02:59.840 --> 03:03.030
It's going to be 2 times
x to the first power.

03:03.030 --> 03:04.930
We could just say 2x.

03:04.930 --> 03:08.690
Now what's interesting is what
we're doing right over here.

03:08.690 --> 03:12.900
The derivative with
respect to x of y squared.

03:12.900 --> 03:17.209
And the realization here is
to just apply the chain rule.

03:17.209 --> 03:19.000
If we're taking the
derivative with respect

03:19.000 --> 03:22.410
to x of this
something, we just have

03:22.410 --> 03:24.930
to take the derivative--
let me make it clear--

03:24.930 --> 03:27.690
we're just going to take the
derivative of our something.

03:27.690 --> 03:31.380
The derivative of y squared--
that's what we're taking,

03:31.380 --> 03:36.270
you can kind of view that as
a function-- with respect to y

03:36.270 --> 03:41.760
and then multiply that
times the derivative of y

03:41.760 --> 03:43.600
with respect to x.

03:43.600 --> 03:47.170
We're assuming that y does
change with respect to x. y

03:47.170 --> 03:49.020
is not some type of
a constant that we're

03:49.020 --> 03:51.260
writing just an abstract terms.

03:51.260 --> 03:53.530
So we're taking the
derivative of this whole thing

03:53.530 --> 03:56.530
with respect to y.

03:56.530 --> 03:58.780
Once again, just the chain rule.

03:58.780 --> 04:01.210
And then we're taking
the derivative of y

04:01.210 --> 04:04.479
with respect to x.

04:04.479 --> 04:06.770
It might be a little bit
clearer if you kind of thought

04:06.770 --> 04:13.560
of it as the derivative
with respect to x of y,

04:13.560 --> 04:14.590
as a function of x.

04:17.860 --> 04:21.647
This might be, or y is
a function of x squared,

04:21.647 --> 04:24.230
which is essentially another way
of writing what we have here.

04:24.230 --> 04:25.820
This might be a
little bit clearer

04:25.820 --> 04:27.640
in terms of the chain rule.

04:27.640 --> 04:29.840
The derivative of
y is a function

04:29.840 --> 04:33.000
of x squared with
respect to y of x.

04:33.000 --> 04:37.140
So the derivative of
something squared with respect

04:37.140 --> 04:40.740
to that something, times the
derivative of that something,

04:40.740 --> 04:42.030
with respect to x.

04:42.030 --> 04:43.580
This is just the chain rule.

04:43.580 --> 04:46.770
I want to say it
over and over again.

04:46.770 --> 04:49.860
This is just the chain rule.

04:49.860 --> 04:50.690
So let's do that.

04:50.690 --> 04:52.710
What do we get on the
right hand side over here?

04:52.710 --> 04:54.420
And I'll write it
over here as well.

04:54.420 --> 04:56.860
This would be equal to the
derivative of y squared

04:56.860 --> 05:03.030
with respect to y, is just
going to be 2 times y.

05:03.030 --> 05:05.690
2 times y, just an
application of the chain rule.

05:05.690 --> 05:07.550
And the derivative of
y with respect to x?

05:07.550 --> 05:08.955
Well, we don't
know what that is.

05:08.955 --> 05:11.330
So we're just going to leave
that as times the derivative

05:11.330 --> 05:14.974
of y with respect to x.

05:14.974 --> 05:16.640
So let's just write
this down over here.

05:16.640 --> 05:25.510
So we have is 2x plus the
derivative of something

05:25.510 --> 05:28.230
squared, with respect to
that something, is 2 times

05:28.230 --> 05:29.160
the something.

05:29.160 --> 05:32.310
In this case, the something
is y, so 2 times y.

05:32.310 --> 05:38.410
And then times the derivative
of y with respect to x.

05:38.410 --> 05:43.030
And this is all going
to be equal to 0.

05:43.030 --> 05:44.720
Now that was interesting.

05:44.720 --> 05:47.490
Now we have an equation
that has the derivative

05:47.490 --> 05:49.567
of a y with respect to x in it.

05:49.567 --> 05:51.650
And this is what we
essentially want to solve for.

05:51.650 --> 05:55.180
This is the slope of the
tangent line at any point.

05:55.180 --> 05:57.370
So all we have to do
at this point is solve

05:57.370 --> 05:59.720
for the derivative of
y with respect to x.

05:59.720 --> 06:01.360
Solve this equation.

06:01.360 --> 06:02.310
So let's do that.

06:02.310 --> 06:04.900
And actually just so we
can do this whole thing

06:04.900 --> 06:08.860
on the same page so we can see
where we started, let me copy

06:08.860 --> 06:11.070
and paste this up here.

06:11.070 --> 06:12.730
This is where we left off.

06:12.730 --> 06:14.620
And let's continue there.

06:14.620 --> 06:17.540
So let's say let's subtract
2x from both sides.

06:17.540 --> 06:22.220
So we're left with 2y
times the derivative of y,

06:22.220 --> 06:25.920
with respect to x, is equal
to-- we're subtracting 2x

06:25.920 --> 06:30.021
from both sides-- so it's
equal to negative 2x.

06:30.021 --> 06:32.270
And if we really want to
solve for the derivative of y

06:32.270 --> 06:35.050
with respect to x, we can
just divide both sides by 2y.

06:39.810 --> 06:42.840
And we're left with
the derivative of y

06:42.840 --> 06:43.930
with respect to x.

06:43.930 --> 06:45.340
Let's scroll down a little bit.

06:45.340 --> 06:47.540
The derivative of
y with respect to x

06:47.540 --> 06:51.930
is equal to, well
the 2s cancel out.

06:51.930 --> 06:59.070
We we're left with
negative x over y.

06:59.070 --> 07:00.410
So this is interesting.

07:00.410 --> 07:02.980
We didn't have to us
explicitly define y

07:02.980 --> 07:04.550
as a function of x here.

07:04.550 --> 07:08.710
But we got our derivative
in terms of an x and a y.

07:08.710 --> 07:10.740
Not just only in terms of an x.

07:10.740 --> 07:12.190
But what does this mean?

07:12.190 --> 07:14.660
Well, if we wanted
to find, let's

07:14.660 --> 07:18.980
say we wanted to find the
derivative at this point

07:18.980 --> 07:20.172
right over here.

07:20.172 --> 07:22.130
Which, if you're familiar
with the unit circle,

07:22.130 --> 07:25.950
so if this was a
45 degree angle,

07:25.950 --> 07:28.610
this would be the square
root of 2 over 2 comma

07:28.610 --> 07:31.090
the square root of 2 over 2.

07:31.090 --> 07:33.830
What is the slope of
the tangent line there?

07:33.830 --> 07:34.830
Well, we figured it out.

07:34.830 --> 07:38.480
It's going to be
negative x over y.

07:38.480 --> 07:46.985
So the slope of the
tangent line here

07:46.985 --> 07:49.050
is going to be
equal to negative x.

07:49.050 --> 07:53.930
So negative square root
of 2 over 2 over y.

07:53.930 --> 07:57.990
Over square root of 2 over 2,
which is equal to negative 1.

07:57.990 --> 08:01.580
And that looks just about right.
